  "response": "For much of the twentieth century, the origin of mitochondria was framed as a choice between two incompatible scenarios. Either mitochondria originated from a free-living bacterium engulfed by an ancestral host cell, or they originated autogenously from membrane compartments formed inside the host cell. The two accounts cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Comparative genomic studies decades later determined that the first scenario, an endosymbiotic origin from an engulfed bacterium, is the one that holds. The endosymbiotic account required that a once-independent organism be retained and progressively integrated into its host. The autogenous account required instead that the host elaborate its own internal membranes into energy-transducing organelles. Resolving the dispute therefore demanded evidence capable of distinguishing an inherited bacterial lineage from a structure derived from the host itself.\n\nThe first such evidence came from the organelle's own molecular machinery. Mitochondria possess their own genome; that is, they carry DNA that is separate from the nuclear genome. Mitochondrial DNA was first discovered in 1985, and that discovery came before the subsequent demonstration that mitochondrial DNA is typically circular in many organisms. That circular architecture in turn causes mitochondrial ribosomes to be sensitive to antibiotics such as chloramphenicol that inhibit bacterial ribosomes. These ribosomes therefore respond to drugs as bacterial ribosomes do, whereas in their structure mitochondrial ribosomes are of the 80S eukaryotic cytoplasmic type. This mixture of bacterial and eukaryotic features gave each camp material to work with. Proponents of endosymbiosis emphasized the pharmacological profile, while their opponents read the ribosomal structure as a sign of the host's own construction. Neither the genome nor the translation apparatus, examined in isolation, could close the question.\n\nSequence comparison sharpened it considerably. Ribosomal RNA phylogenies place mitochondria within the Alphaproteobacteria. Although that phylogenetic placement points toward a specific bacterial lineage, Rudolf Raff and Henry Mahler defended a non-symbiotic, autogenous origin of mitochondria in 1975. Their case rested on the claim that features shared between organelles and bacteria could reflect convergent adaptation or gene transfer into compartments the host had already built. On their view, molecular resemblance did not by itself demonstrate descent. The argument was influential precisely because it accepted the molecular observations while disputing the inference drawn from them. Whole-genome data then entered the debate. The 1998 genome sequence of Rickettsia prowazekii revealed close kinship with mitochondria, exposing a large set of genes and pathways shared between an intracellular parasite and the organelle.\n\nComparative phylogenomic analyses ultimately determined that the autogenous hypothesis could not account for the bacterial ancestry of the mitochondrial genome. That determination settles the tension between their 1975 argument and the ribosomal trees. It also confirms the alphaproteobacterial placement that the rRNA phylogenies had recovered. Once many genes, rather than a single molecule, converged on the same lineage, convergence and piecemeal transfer could no longer explain the pattern. Attention accordingly shifted from whether a bacterium was involved to what kind of bacterium it had been. The mitochondrial ancestor was capable of aerobic respiration or capable of anaerobic metabolism; at least one of these capacities held, perhaps both. Each option bears on the ecological setting in which the host and the bacterium first came together, and each continues to inform models of early eukaryotic energetics.\n\nA 1999 review in Science by Gray, Burger, and Lang concluded that mitochondria descend from a single alphaproteobacterial ancestor. That review drew together organellar genome sequences from diverse eukaryotes with the bacterial genomes then becoming available. It showed that the mitochondrial gene content observed across lineages could be traced to one founding acquisition rather than to several independent events. The single-origin conclusion completed the shift in the field. The endosymbiotic account became the framework for further inquiry, and the autogenous account came to be studied for its historical role in sharpening the evidence rather than as a viable model of how mitochondria arose.",
